import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 641 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 642 | 2026-10-24 | **Rhythm on the Home, part 1 (her order: Pitch gaps done, then Rhythm).** Beat and time is now a live Home concept with two groups, on the same lesson engine as Pitch (whole picture, one at a time, your turn). New: RqTap, the listen-and-tap figure (one bar drawn like the book, NvBar; ▶ Listen plays the notes with the click; ▶ Tap it gives 4 clicks to get ready, then the student taps every note, or every beat, on the pad or the space bar; scored on the audio clock with plMatch; a long row is drawn as bars of 4), and a scored 'tap' question kind (right = every note on time, no extra taps; try again as often as needed). Group 1 'The steady beat' (Questions: pulse drill): The steady beat (like a heartbeat, tap with the click, steady or not?), Count out loud (1 2 3 4; the click stops after 4 beats, keep going), Silent beats still count (rests: count, do not tap; which beat was silent?), Beat or rhythm? (tap every heart vs every sound, walk and run-ning). Group 2 'How long notes last' (Questions: note-values drill): The crotchet: one beat, The minim: two beats, Three and four beats (dotted minim, semibreve), Two quavers on one beat (1 +), Rests: silence you count; each with tap-it, how many beats?, which bar did you hear? or which rest matches?. Counting words kept as the app has them: 1 2 3 4, 1 +; walk / run-ning (타 / 티-티); asked her (00:58 message) whether she uses other words. Groups 3-5 (bars and time signatures, rhythm patterns, more time) next. Files: drills.html, drills.src.html, _workspace/concept-book-kit/sc/{patch664.py,rq.jsx}. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
